{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>And Moses brought near, etc.</b> It is possible that the verse intends according to the words of Tanna deChakhamim and so forth that I cited in connection with the verse ״לָמָּה יִגָּרַע״ — that they argued: if we are as a son, we inherit; and if not, let our mother be taken in levirate marriage. So too did Moses present [the matter] before HaShem. And his reason for saying thus was so that if HaShem were to tell him they have no inheritance, He would also give him a reason why their mother should not be taken in levirate marriage." "It may further be explained according to what they said in Bava Batra (119:) — and this is their language: Rabbi Chidka said: Shimon haShikmoni was a colleague of mine from the disciples of Rabbi Akiva, and thus he would say — Moses knew that the daughters of Tzelofchad would inherit, but he did not know whether they take the portion of the firstborn. And the passage of inheritances was fit to be written by the hand of Moses, except that the daughters of Tzelofchad merited, and it was written through them. And the passage of the wood-gatherer and so forth was fit [to be written by Moses], except that the wood-gatherer incurred liability, and it was written through him — end quote. And Tosafot there wrote in the passage beginning ״Moses knew, etc.״: even though the passage of inheritances was not stated, he knew from the reasoning: if as a son — we inherit; if as a daughter — let our mother be taken in levirate marriage, as below (Numbers 27:4) — end quote." "<b>And it is difficult</b> according to this baraita, [in light of] that baraita (Zevachim 115:) in which Rabbi Akiva and Rabbi Yishmael disagreed concerning the order of the giving of the Torah — for they all agree that the general principles of the Torah and its particulars were stated at Sinai. And according to this baraita, it was not said to Moses whether they take the portion of the firstborn or not until the fortieth year, when he was in the plains of Moav after the death of Aaron, for thus the verse says ״before Elazar,״ from which you learn that Aaron had died. This is fine regarding the passage of the wood-gatherer, since it was in the first year, as they said of blessed memory (Sifrei ch. 1, 113:) — we can say that HaShem had stated it to Moses at Sinai, except that the wood-gatherer incurred liability and so forth — but not so regarding what is before us. And furthermore, according to this, Aaron died lacking knowledge of the Torah in the specifics of this law." "<b>And it appears</b> that in any event all the laws of the Torah were stated to Moses at Sinai — general principles and particulars — and so too the passage of inheritances, and Moses and Aaron knew that the daughters would also take the portion of the firstborn. And what they said in the baraita, that he did not know whether they would take the portion of the firstborn — the meaning is concerning the land of Israel. The intention is whether it is considered ra'uy (expected) or muchzak (held possession), for the firstborn takes a double portion only in muchzak and not in ra'uy. And this matter did not arise from a deficiency in knowledge of the halakhah but rather from lack of knowledge of the factual reality — whether [the land] is called muchzak or ra'uy. And lack of knowledge of this matter is not a deficiency in the laws of the Torah. And so the matter is also proven from what they said there in the Gemara, which raises a difficulty from this baraita of Rabbi Shimon haShikmoni to Rava, who said that the land of Israel is muchzakah. And [the Gemara] said: and if it enters your mind that it is muchzakah, what is he uncertain about? And it answers: that very thing is what he is uncertain about, in the meaning of the verse (Exodus 6:8) ״ונתתי אתה לכם מורשה״ and so forth. Meaning, if the meaning of morashah is yerushah (inheritance), and according to this the firstborn would take a double portion in it — or perhaps the meaning of morashah is that they would be morishin (transmitting) it. And it was resolved for him: yerushah lahem (an inheritance to you) and morishin levanhem (they transmit to their children). And that is what is written (Exodus 15:17): ״תביאמו ותטעמו״ and so forth — teaching that they prophesy and do not know what, and so forth — end quote. These matters demonstrate that the uncertainty was about the land, not about the law of the firstborn, since the passage of inheritances had already been stated. For if not so, what is the Talmud's difficulty in what Moses was uncertain about, given that the passage of inheritances had not yet been stated until that hour?" "<b>And it is also explained</b> from the Gemara that also the law of the land of Israel — whether it is muchzakah or ra'uyah — was stated in the Torah, except that Moses was uncertain about the meaning of morashah. And even that uncertainty has a clarification in the Torah, from the verse ״תביאמו ותטעמו״ (Exodus 15:17), but he did not sense it, as they said: they prophesy and do not know, and so forth. And from this, the verse's saying <b>and Moses brought near their judgment</b> is the claim of the firstborn's portion in the land. And the Merciful One said to him <b>and you shall transfer the inheritance of</b> and so forth, and He explained there that one seeks the hint of the firstborn's portion from the word ״והעברת,״ as it is written (Exodus 13:12) ״והעברת כל פטר רחם.״ And although you will find that the entire subject of inheritances is written here, in its time it was stated at Sinai but was fixed here — in the manner that they said of blessed memory (Sanhedrin 59:) regarding circumcision and the displaced sinew (Chullin 100:), that they were stated at Sinai but were fixed there. However, there remains for us to know where the daughters hinted in their words at the claim of the firstborn's portion, concerning which it says that Moses brought near their judgment. And perhaps there is no need for them to have asked [explicitly] — if the judge knows that they have a right to something not mentioned in their words and he is uncertain about it, he may inquire of the God of judgment. Or it is possible that they hinted at it in the statement <b>Why should the name of our father be diminished</b> — for they wanted the matter to be for them as though their father were the one entering the land. And the matter is clear that their intent was to say that they should take the portion of the firstborn in the land. And according to this, the verse's saying <b>their judgment</b> means — the judgment that they demanded concerning the portion of the firstborn."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :register {:flags {4 ["God"]}}, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:15:39.744905112Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}